motions.motion_text:
WHEREAS the corner lot located at the entrance to Dunrobin Village at 2802 Dunrobin Rd. has served as a de facto public gathering space and community amenity for many years;
AND WHEREAS the Dunrobin and District Women’s Institute has dedicated more than a decade to enhancing and maintaining the site through volunteer efforts, including the installation and upkeep of gardens, tree plantings, public benches, a community “Little Library,” a memorial plaque recognizing the Younghusband family, and the annual Lighting of the Christmas Tree celebration;
AND WHEREAS the site has become a focal point for community activities and informal recreation, providing a place for residents, visitors, cyclists, families, and dog walkers to gather, rest, and socialize;
AND WHEREAS the corner lot has been used as a community viewing area during local celebrations, including Canada Day festivities and fireworks held at the Dunrobin Community Centre;
AND WHEREAS the site serves as an important gateway feature and visual entrance to Dunrobin Village, contributing positively to the character, identity, and appearance of the community;
AND WHEREAS community volunteers have experienced challenges accessing municipal programs, grants, conservation initiatives, and other supports due to the site's current designation and status;
AND WHEREAS many residents view the property as a public park and community green space and have expressed a desire to see its long-term protection and preservation for public use;
AND WHEREAS the City of Ottawa has identified the importance of providing and preserving parks, open spaces, and recreational opportunities within rural communities;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED THAT staff be directed to review the municipally-owned corner lot at 2802 Dunrobin Road, located at the entrance to Dunrobin Village and report back to the Agriculture and Rural Affairs Committee on options to designate the property as parkland and incorporate it into the City's parkland inventory;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT the staff review include an assessment of any planning, environmental, zoning, operational, financial, and parkland acquisition implications associated with redesignating the property for park purposes;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT staff consult with the local Ward Councillor, the Dunrobin and Women’s Institute, the Dunrobin Community Association, and the broader community as part of this review process and report back with recommendations.
